    slug: 'its-never-the-carburetor',
    title: 'It\u2019s Never the Carburetor',
    date: '2026-07-16',
    author: 'Jeff Ritter',
    summary: 'When a vintage motorcycle won\u2019t start, the owner always blames the carburetor. When a college is in trouble, the board always blames enrollment. Both are wrong \u2014 and the real problem has been getting worse while everyone was looking at the wrong part.',
    body: `When a vintage BMW motorcycle won\u2019t start, the owner always says the same thing: \u201cI think it\u2019s the carburetor.\u201d

It\u2019s never the carburetor.

It\u2019s the points. It\u2019s the condenser. It\u2019s a cracked vacuum line you can\u2019t see, or a petcock that\u2019s been slowly failing for two years, or a ground wire that corroded last winter while the bike sat in the garage under a tarp. The carburetor is the thing people can name, so it\u2019s the thing people blame. The actual problem is quieter, less obvious, and has been getting worse while everyone was looking at the wrong part.

I rode BMW motorcycles for most of my adult life \u2014 never rebuilt one, despite telling myself every winter that this was the year \u2014 but I\u2019ve stood in enough garages, watched enough real mechanics work, and broken down on enough back roads to know what a misdiagnosis looks like. And I\u2019ve been thinking about carburetors a lot lately \u2014 because I spend my days now talking to college presidents and board members, and they all say the same thing.

\u201cIt\u2019s enrollment.\u201d

It\u2019s never enrollment.

**Enrollment is the carburetor.** It\u2019s the visible symptom, the thing everyone can name, the number on the first slide of every board presentation. Enrollment is down, so we need to recruit harder, discount deeper, add a nursing program, launch an esports team, build a new student center, hire a VP of Enrollment Management with a capital-letter title and a six-figure salary. Fix the carburetor and the bike will start.

The bike doesn\u2019t start.

**Here\u2019s what\u2019s actually wrong:**

The revenue model is a single-cylinder engine in a world that requires eight. A college that depends on tuition from 18-year-olds for 85% of its revenue is running on one cylinder \u2014 and that cylinder is shrinking every year as the demographic cliff steepens, as public skepticism of the degree grows, as families calculate the ROI on a $180,000 investment and increasingly say no.

Enrollment strategies don\u2019t fix a revenue model. They optimize a broken one. You can recruit your way to a bigger freshman class and still lose money on every student because your discount rate is 60% and your cost structure was built for an enrollment you\u2019ll never see again. Iowa Wesleyan\u2019s enrollment was *rising* when it closed. The admissions team did its job. The engine was still broken.

**The points are corroded.** That\u2019s the board culture. Most boards of struggling colleges are populated by alumni who love the institution as it was, not as it needs to become. They joined to preserve, not to transform. Every meeting is a referendum on whether things are \u201cas bad as all that,\u201d and the answer is always \u201cnot yet\u201d \u2014 until the meeting where the answer is \u201cit\u2019s too late.\u201d A board that asks \u201chow do we get enrollment back to where it was?\u201d is cleaning the carburetor. A board that asks \u201cwhat should this campus become?\u201d is checking the ignition.

**The vacuum line is cracked.** That\u2019s the gap between what the campus offers and what the community needs. A 101-acre campus in a town with 25% poverty and a manufacturing plant ten miles away that can\u2019t find welders is a vacuum line disconnected from the engine. The campus has classrooms, workshops, dormitories, dining halls, and green space. The community needs workforce training, childcare, health services, business incubation, and a gathering place. The two are sitting right next to each other, and nobody\u2019s made the connection \u2014 because the college is too busy trying to recruit 18-year-olds from three states away to notice the 30-year-olds across the street who need a CDL.

**The petcock has been failing for years.** That\u2019s the financial structure \u2014 the slow leak everyone can hear if they listen. Deferred maintenance growing by $2 million a year. Endowment draws increasing from 5% to 7% to \u201cwhatever we need this quarter.\u201d Staff positions eliminated and never refilled, so the remaining staff absorb the work until they leave too. The petcock doesn\u2019t fail dramatically. It drips. And one day you turn the key and the tank is dry and everyone acts surprised.

**The ground wire corroded over the winter.** That\u2019s the loss of community trust \u2014 the quietest failure and the hardest to repair. When a campus stops engaging its town, when the president stops showing up at Rotary, when the college raises tuition while the town\u2019s median income stays flat, when the dining hall goes dark on weekends and the gym locks its doors to non-students \u2014 the ground wire corrodes. And when the college finally needs the community to rally, to write to the legislature, to show up at the accreditor\u2019s hearing, to support a transformation plan \u2014 the connection isn\u2019t there anymore. It corroded while the bike was under the tarp.

**Here\u2019s what I\u2019ve learned from motorcycles and colleges:**

The owner who comes to you saying \u201cit\u2019s the carburetor\u201d isn\u2019t wrong about the symptom. The bike won\u2019t start. But if you clean the carburetor and hand it back, you haven\u2019t fixed anything \u2014 you\u2019ve just given them a clean carburetor on a bike that still won\u2019t run. And they\u2019ll be back in a month, angrier, with less trust, and less money.

The mechanic\u2019s job is to say, respectfully but clearly: \u201cIt\u2019s not the carburetor. Let me show you what it actually is.\u201d

That\u2019s what we do at Campus Transformation. We don\u2019t fix the carburetor. We look at the whole machine \u2014 the revenue model, the board culture, the community connection, the financial structure, the physical assets, the workforce demand within driving distance \u2014 and we tell you what\u2019s actually broken. Sometimes the answer is that the college can be saved. Sometimes the answer is that the college can\u2019t, but the campus can \u2014 and should \u2014 become something bigger, something that serves more people, something that runs on eight cylinders instead of one.

The hardest part isn\u2019t the diagnosis. Any good mechanic can find the real problem in an hour. The hardest part is getting the owner to stop pointing at the carburetor.

If your board is having the carburetor conversation \u2014 \u201cwe just need more students, we just need a better marketing plan, we just need one more year\u201d \u2014 it might be time to let someone look at the whole engine.

The first conversation is free. And I promise you, it\u2019s not the carburetor.

*Jeff Ritter is the founder of Campus Transformation. He spent 25 years as a professor and a lifetime riding vintage BMW motorcycles \u2014 though \u201crebuilding\u201d is generous. He watched other people rebuild them, asked a lot of questions, handed them the wrong wrench at critical moments, and once successfully changed a spark plug that didn\u2019t need changing. But he rode every model BMW made between 1965 and 2003, broke down on most of them, and learned more about diagnostics from the side of the road than any shop manual could teach. The metaphor stands. The mechanical credentials do not.*`
